# Implementation plans are ephemeral build scaffolding (superpowers:writing-plans).
# Specs (docs/superpowers/specs/) are durable and tracked; plans are not — once a
# plan's work merges, the spec + CHANGELOG + code carry everything durable.
docs/superpowers/plans/

# Session-local runtime scaffolding (scheduled-task locks, etc.) — never tracked.
.claude/

# Agent-runtime working directories. `.claude/` above already covers one of them; the
# others arrived later and were untracked AND unignored, which in a PUBLIC repo is one
# `git add -A` away from publishing them. `.hermes/` alone holds ~1.9 MB of candidate
# tar.gz archives for the dormant Rust engine, agent working logs, and gate reports —
# none of it part of the project, all of it reconstructible from the issue tracker.
.hermes/
.multica/
.agent_context/
.DS_Store

# Dormant Rust engine build output — never track. Source (engine/src, engine/tests,
# engine/corpus) IS tracked; only the compiler's artifacts and the Python linter's
# cache stay out, so a plain `git add -A` after a build cannot publish binaries.
engine/target/
engine/.ruff_cache/

# Python bytecode caches from the dormant Hermes adapter (smoke, py_compile) —
# regenerated, never source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
